WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.280
Reading loss curves.

2
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:07.981
Log the training loss every few steps and evaluate on a held-out validation set at regular intervals.

3
00:00:08.261 --> 00:00:22.704
TRL logs the mean loss over scored tokens, plus the mean token accuracy (how often the model’s top prediction is the target token) and the entropy of its predictions, which helps tell confident from uncertain models.

4
00:00:22.984 --> 00:00:24.669
Then read the two curves together.

5
00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:26.549
Both falling, then flattening.

6
00:00:26.829 --> 00:00:27.576
Normal.

7
00:00:27.856 --> 00:00:33.125
The gap between them is the generalization gap; a small, stable gap is what you want.

8
00:00:33.405 --> 00:00:36.371
Training keeps falling, validation turns upward.

9
00:00:36.651 --> 00:00:41.301
Overfitting: the model is fitting training examples in ways that don’t transfer.

10
00:00:41.581 --> 00:00:46.829
Prefer an earlier checkpoint, fewer epochs, a lower learning rate, or more varied data.

11
00:00:47.109 --> 00:00:50.224
A staircase in the training loss at each epoch boundary.

12
00:00:50.504 --> 00:00:52.189
Howard and Whitaker at fast.

13
00:00:52.469 --> 00:01:04.928
ai (2023) reported sharp drops at the start of each new epoch when fine-tuning LLMs, which they read as the model memorizing examples after a single exposure.

14
00:01:05.208 --> 00:01:13.165
In their experiments, validation loss rose while validation accuracy kept improving, which they attributed to overconfidence.

15
00:01:13.445 --> 00:01:22.277
They framed this as early work, but the practical lesson holds: a rising validation loss is a prompt to check the task metric, not a verdict.

16
00:01:22.557 --> 00:01:23.560
A flat line.

17
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.536
Learning rate too low, the wrong parameters frozen, or (common) almost every label masked.

18
00:01:29.816 --> 00:01:31.907
Decode one batch’s labels and look.

19
00:01:32.187 --> 00:01:33.445
Spikes or NaN.

20
00:01:33.725 --> 00:01:38.824
Learning rate too high, mixed-precision overflow, or an example with nothing to score.

21
00:01:39.104 --> 00:01:42.069
Training loss near zero within a few dozen steps.

22
00:01:42.349 --> 00:01:48.749
Either the task is trivially templated (your lab’s synthetic data is), or the answers leak into the prompts.

23
00:01:49.029 --> 00:01:52.101
Check for duplicates between splits before celebrating.
